---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbNode, faqNode, pageGraph, webPageNode } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'Insurance while an RV or boat is stored | RV Storage Finder';
const description =
  'NAIC’s June 11, 2026 article says an auto policy will not cover an RV. This page quotes that and says what it does not decide about a vehicle in storage.';
const path = '/insurance/';
const checked = '2026-11-19';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { label: 'Insurance', path },
];
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Does the storage lot’s insurance cover the RV or the boat?',
    a: 'This page did not find an NAIC sentence that says it does. The rental agreement is the lot’s contract. The vehicle policy is a separate contract. Ask the facility what the agreement says it will not pay for, and ask the insurer whether the policy applies at that address.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does comprehensive coverage on a car cover an RV parked at the lot?',
    a: 'NAIC’s June 11, 2026 article says comprehensive pays for non-collision damage to a car, and the same article says an auto policy will not cover an RV. The car sentence is not a promise about the coach. Read the policy that names the RV.',
  },
];
const schema = pageGraph([
  webPageNode({ title, description, path, dateModified: checked }),
  breadcrumbNode(crumbs),
  faqNode(faqs),
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Insurance while an RV or boat is stored</h1>
    <p>
      This page quotes the National Association of Insurance Commissioners. It is not insurance advice,
      not a reading of a policy, and not a statement that a storage lot insures the vehicle. No listing
      in this directory has an insurance field. A gate, a camera, or a roof is not coverage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checked <time datetime={checked}>November 19, 2026</time>. The consumer article below is dated
      June 11, 2026. The boat definition is from the NAIC glossary as posted that day. The product-code
      sentence is from the 2021 matrix file still posted on the NAIC site. This page does not claim a
      later matrix replaced it.
    </p>

    <h2>An auto policy and an RV</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://content.naic.org/article/what-does-auto-insurance-cover" rel="noopener noreferrer">“What Does Auto Insurance Cover?”</a>
      says, under what is not covered:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Specialty vehicles. Your auto policy will not cover motorcycles, all-terrain vehicles (ATVs),
        off-road vehicles, recreational vehicles (RVs), or commercial vehicles.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The same article describes comprehensive coverage for a car: “Comprehensive coverage pays for
      damage to your car that’s not caused by a collision (wreck or crash), like theft, hail,
      windstorm, flood, fire, or impact by an animal.” That sentence says car. It sits in the same
      article as the sentence that an auto policy will not cover an RV. This page does not treat the
      car sentence as coverage for a stored coach.
    </p>
    <p>
      A separate NAIC file, the
      <a href="https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/2021%20PC%20PCM.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">2021 Uniform Property &amp; Casualty Product Coding Matrix</a>,
      gives RV policies their own code, 19.0003:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        RV filings (including filings for Golf Carts) which include singularly or in any combination
        coverage such as the following: Auto Liability, PIP, MP, Uninsured Motorist and/or Underinsured
        Motorists (UM/UIM); Specified Causes of loss, Comprehensive, and Collision.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      That sentence describes how an RV filing can be coded. It does not say a particular policy stays
      in force at a storage lot, and it does not say an open lot and an enclosed building are the same
      risk. It also does not undo the consumer sentence: the auto policy that covers a car is not, on
      that article’s words, the policy that covers the RV.
    </p>

    <h2>A boat</h2>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="https://content.naic.org/glossary-insurance-terms" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAIC glossary</a>
      defines the boatowners line this way:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Boatowners/Personal Watercraft — covers damage to pleasure boats, motors, trailers, boating
        equipment, and personal watercraft as well as bodily injury and property damage liability to
        others.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      That is a description of the line of business. It names the boat, the motor, the trailer, and the
      equipment. It does not say the coverage applies while the boat sits in a lot, and it does not say
      the tow vehicle’s auto policy includes the hull. Ask which contract names the boat.
    </p>

    <h2>Household goods in a storage unit</h2>
    <p>
      NAIC’s article
      <a href="https://content.naic.org/article/consumer-insight-moving-places-changing-spaces" rel="noopener noreferrer">“Moving Places, Changing Spaces,”</a>
      dated December 1, 2015, has a different storage sentence. The page’s date-modified field read
      January 16, 2024. Under “Storing Your Possessions” it says:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        If you’re putting your items in storage during the relocation, talk to your insurer about what
        coverage you will have for your possessions while they are in the facility. You might need to
        purchase a policy known as an inland marine policy or floater to provide you the coverage you
        need while your possessions are being stored.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Those possessions are household goods during a move. The sentence is not a rule for an RV or a
      boat, and this page does not turn it into one.
    </p>

    <h2>The lot’s paperwork is a second contract</h2>
    <p>
      None of the NAIC pages above says a storage facility’s insurance covers the customer’s vehicle.
      The rental agreement is a contract with the lot. The vehicle policy is a contract with an insurer.
      A listing that says gated, cameras, or covered is not either contract.
    </p>
    <p>Take these questions to the policy and to the facility. This page does not answer them.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Which policy is supposed to cover this RV or this boat? If the only policy names a car, read the specialty-vehicle sentence again.</li>
      <li>Does that policy apply while the vehicle is stored, and does the address or the kind of space change the answer?</li>
      <li>Which of theft, hail, wind, flood, and fire does the policy name, and what deductible does it print?</li>
      <li>Are contents, a dinghy, and electronics included, or only the vehicle named on the declarations page?</li>
      <li>Does the policy require notice of where the vehicle is kept?</li>
      <li>Does the rental agreement require proof of insurance, and does the facility’s own policy name this vehicle?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A state insurance regulator, not this directory, takes complaints about an insurer. NAIC lists
      those departments at
      <a href="https://content.naic.org/state-insurance-departments" rel="noopener noreferrer">state insurance departments</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What this page does not decide</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It does not quote a Florida, Nevada, Arizona, Texas, Georgia, or North Carolina insurance statute. Those codes were not the source.</li>
      <li>It does not say that storing a vehicle keeps coverage, or that storing a vehicle voids it.</li>
      <li>It does not state a premium, a deductible, or a lay-up credit. Those numbers are on a policy, if they are anywhere.</li>
      <li>A facility listing remains a storage lot. It is not proof of insurance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={href('/checklists/security/')}>Security checklist</a>, which says a gate and cameras do not replace a policy</li>
      <li><a href={href('/checklists/seasonal/')}>Seasonal checklist</a>, including the step to confirm a stored vehicle is still covered</li>
      <li><a href={href('/guide/')}>How to read a price, a gate hour, and a camera line</a></li>
    </ul>

    <section class="faq">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
      {
        faqs.map((faq) => (
          <>
            <h3>{faq.q}</h3>
            <p>{faq.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
